 8\, 2021) (codified at 15 M.R.S. § 5826 (2026))\; P.L. 2023\, ch. 196\, \
 n§ 1 (effective Oct. 25\, 2023) (codified at 15 M.R.S. § 5826 (2026)). \
 n 2 \nmotion.  Belony entered a conditional guilty plea and now appeals fr
 om the \njudgment of conviction\, challenging the court’s denial of his 
 motion to suppress.   \n[¶2]  Belony puts forward three arguments in supp
 ort of his assertion \nthat the court erred in refusing to suppress the ev
 idence: (1) law enforcement \nlacked a reasonable\, articulable suspicion 
 to stop the vehicle\; (2)  law \nenforcement prolonged the traffic stop be
 yond what was reasonable\; and \n(3) law enforcement lacked probable cause
  to search Belony’s person.  Because \nwe conclude that the trial court 
 did not make sufficient findings to support the \nreasonableness of the in
 vestigatory stop’s duration\, we vacate the judgment of \nconviction and
  vacate the order denying Belony’s m\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/court
